WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.031
Every outbound HTTP request crosses a trust boundary.

2
00:00:03.031 --> 00:00:11.464
The Python application chooses a destination, resolves a name, opens a
connection, sends headers and data, and accepts a response from another
system.

3
00:00:11.464 --> 00:00:19.371
That destination may be a configured internal service, a partner API, an
identity provider, a storage endpoint, or a user-selected callback.

4
00:00:19.371 --> 00:00:25.498
The security question is who can influence the destination and whether the
application is allowed to reach it.

5
00:00:25.498 --> 00:00:29.122
Untrusted input should not freely choose network destinations.

6
00:00:29.122 --> 00:00:42.431
User-supplied URLs, callback addresses, file fetch targets, webhook
destinations, or database-stored endpoint fields may appear legitimate
while still sending the server toward an unintended public, private, or
sensitive location.

7
00:00:42.431 --> 00:00:51.392
A safer pattern is to map user choices to approved destination records,
allowlisted hosts, expected schemes, known ports, and reviewed service
configuration.

8
00:00:51.392 --> 00:00:58.046
Destination checks need to account for name resolution, redirects,
proxies, and environment behavior at a high level.

9
00:00:58.046 --> 00:01:00.616
A host name may resolve differently over time.

10
00:01:00.616 --> 00:01:02.197
A proxy may change the route.

11
00:01:02.197 --> 00:01:05.558
A redirect may move the request to a different host or scheme.

12
00:01:05.558 --> 00:01:08.918
Private and public address spaces may carry different risk.

13
00:01:08.918 --> 00:01:16.824
The application should define what is allowed before a request starts and
verify that automatic behavior does not silently cross boundaries.

14
00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:22.095
Outbound request control reduces accidental exposure of internal services
and credentials.

15
00:01:22.095 --> 00:01:23.940
It also improves observability.

16
00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:31.056
When a Python service is expected to contact only known systems, logs and
alerts can identify unusual destinations more easily.

17
00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:34.284
Destination control is not about disabling integration.

18
00:01:34.284 --> 00:01:40.280
It is about making integration explicit, reviewable, and aligned with the
business purpose of the feature.
